<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emancipation Proclamation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Emancipation Proclamation was an executive order issued by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> on 1 January 1863, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, declaring free all enslaved people in the states then in rebellion against the Union. It did not apply to the slave states loyal to the Union or to areas already under Union control, but it made the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> a war aim, opened the Union army and navy to Black men, and discouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from supporting the Confederacy. Slavery was abolished throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> in 1865.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation.jpg" alt="Emancipation Proclamation" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. Roberts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emancipation_Proclamation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians agree there were no slave insurrections, but enslaved people grew more independent, spied for the North and ran in large numbers to federal lines, and after the <b>Emancipation Proclamation</b> of 1 January 1863 over 200,000 freed people worked for the federal army, eventually as soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the American Civil War Abraham Lincoln’s <b>Emancipation Proclamation</b> of 1 January 1863 declared free the enslaved in the rebel states, some 180,000 Black men served in the Union army, and the Thirteenth Amendment, ratified in December 1865, ended slavery throughout the country.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…published in the Chicago Tribune on 16 December 1861; Edna Dean Proctor wrote &quot;The President's Proclamation&quot; for the <b>Emancipation Proclamation</b> in 1863; Captain Lindley Miller wrote, or wrote down, the &quot;Song of the First of Arkansas&quot; in 1864, a similar text being attributed to Sojourner Truth; German veterans…</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li><li><blockquote>His <b>Emancipation Proclamation</b> of a month later made more than 3.5 million people enslaved in the Confederate states legally free.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>General Gordon Granger announced that all enslaved people in Texas were free, two and a half years after the <b>Emancipation Proclamation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Lee's retreat gave Lincoln the victory he needed to announce the <b>Emancipation Proclamation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
